Z||000009||Ed||11-18-2000||11:10 PM||||Well, you didn't respond to ogre's point about proper filling procedure. <P>It IS a big deal.  The highest point in the system is the filler cap.  So, that should be the highest point when you fill up, like in an inclined driveway.  The ONLY time my long-departed and lamented (rear-ended and totaled, but I digress) four banger overheated was when the Pontiac dealer worked on it and didn't follow the correct procedure in refilling it.  It got REALLY hot.  After it was "burped" it was fine and neither that car, nor my current six has ever overheated in the hot California summers.  <P>So, as mentioned in an earlier post, please look for the easy to solve stuff first before declaring you car dead.||||reg||1
Z||000010||sqoach||11-18-2000||11:24 PM||||ed, what is "burping"???? ||||reg||1
Z||000011||WKDFIRO||11-19-2000||12:17 AM||||Don't forget the simple stuff!<P>On a caravan we had a member keep overheating, overflowing from the tank...we stopped over at a autoparts shop and came out with a new radiator cap. The old one could not hold the pressure and would immediately flow into the overflow tank. The coolant would not be in the system and it would over heat. <P>With a new radiator cap he completed a 200 mile trip with no problems!<P>Hope its that simple!||||reg||10
Z||000012||sqoach||11-19-2000||12:30 AM||||you know i was lookin at that cap the other day and wondered about it.  i guess i can just go buy one and find out.  do you know if it's possible that the coolant could be traveling through the system too fast, that it wouldn't cool the radiator down or something?  it sounds kinda weird to me.||||reg||1
Z||000013||RossT||11-19-2000||12:41 AM||||you may have a new water pump, but some rebuilt pumps came with plastic impellars. Its my understanding that they do not flow as well as the one with metal impellars. After you replace rad. cap, and burp the system ( easier to do with fron of car facing down a small hill), run the car and make sure both coolant tubes are hot.  You may also have a clog in the system.<P>also do a search of the forum, or buy cliffs cd, I remember a forum memeber who had the same problem after replacing the water pump, and when he replaced it again with a metal impellar the problem was solved<p>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 11-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||aburnett||11-19-2000||12:55 AM||||I had the same problem with my 84 that has now become my parts car. However it was only a head gasket and not a cracked head. ||||reg||10
Z||000015||Ed||11-19-2000||01:58 AM||||Re: "Burping"<P>The answers to many forum questions, and in particular to this one about the proper coolant filling procedure will be found here:<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/osg.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/osg.html</A> <P>I've been told to run cars with the thermostat in place. <P>The easiest way to check for a cracked head or blown gasket is to watch for white smoke (water vapor) coming out the tailpipe when the car is at the NORMAL operating temperature.  There may be white smoke (water vapor) at start-up because of water in the exhaust pipe.  After everything is warmed up the source of white smoke would be coolant water.  <P>Also look for milky oil which indicates water is getting into the oil. <P>Too advanced timing would also make the engine overheat.  <P>A bad catalytic converter would make the car overheat.  <P>I've had all these problems at different times.<P>[This message has been edited by Ed (edited 11-19-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by Ed (edited 11-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||theogre||11-19-2000||01:43 PM||||If you end up pulling off the head, REPLACE all the head bolts. Do Not reuse them. GM has an acknowledged problem with the head bolts on the 4 cyl. The exhaust side bolts are prone to breaking.<P>A thermostat is required for all EFI motors to operate correctly. It's not an option any more. You choices for a stock programed ECM/PCM are 180 or 195. The car makers all install 195s at the factory for both fuel efficiency and emmisions reasons.||||reg||1
Z||000017||sqoach||11-19-2000||09:12 PM||||well, i think i do need a new catalytic converter cuz i found out there's a hole in it!  ahhhh!  i noticed some smoke coming out from underneath and it was coming from this rusted out hole in my cat.  my exhaust does sputter out some water, but it's really cold here and it's when i first start it.  i'll have to check that again though.  after reading some interesting things about thermostats, i decided getting the 160 was not a good idea.  i figured it would help keep my car cooler.  newayz...i'll give you guys some updates tomorrow most likey.  i think the temp's gonna get above 40.||||reg||11
